The invention concerns an electrical power generation arrangement that includes:

- an alternator (1) configured to be connected to a drive system (7) and to deliver an AC voltage to an output bus (10);
- a bidirectional AC/DC converter (2), whose AC bus (6) is coupled to the alternator’s output bus (10);
- an electrical energy storage component (3) linked to the DC bus (9) of the converter (2); and
- a control unit (4) designed to respond to transient events, such as load shedding or a charging impact.

During load shedding, the controller (4) operates the converter (2) so that energy present on the alternator output bus (10) is captured and stored in the storage element (3). During a charging impact, the controller (4) instead causes energy to be drawn from the storage element (3) and delivered into the output bus (10). The converter (2) is also driven to supply currents into the AC bus (10) of the alternator (1) in order to offset harmonic currents.

counterfactuals.output:
An electrical power generation system comprising:

a prime mover;

an alternator mechanically coupled to and driven by the prime mover, the alternator having an alternating-current output coupled to an output bus;

an electrical energy storage device configured to store direct-current electrical energy;

a bidirectional AC/DC power converter electrically coupled between the output bus and the electrical energy storage device; and

a controller operatively coupled to the bidirectional AC/DC power converter,

wherein the controller is configured to regulate operation of the bidirectional AC/DC power converter during transient operating conditions of the electrical power generation system by selectively operating the bidirectional AC/DC power converter in:

(i) a rectification mode in which electrical energy from the output bus is converted to direct-current electrical energy and supplied to the electrical energy storage device, thereby absorbing excess energy from the output bus; and

(ii) an inversion mode in which direct-current electrical energy from the electrical energy storage device is converted to alternating-current electrical energy and injected into the output bus, thereby supplementing output of the alternator.

In one embodiment, the controller is configured to determine an energy surplus or energy deficit on the output bus based at least in part on one or more sensed parameters including bus voltage, bus frequency, alternator current, load demand, prime mover speed, or state of charge of the electrical energy storage device.

In another embodiment, the controller is configured to command the bidirectional AC/DC power converter to absorb energy from the output bus in response to a load decrease, overspeed condition, overvoltage condition, or overfrequency condition, and to command the bidirectional AC/DC power converter to deliver energy to the output bus in response to a load increase, underspeed condition, undervoltage condition, or underfrequency condition.

A method of managing transient energy in an alternator-based power system comprises:

driving an alternator with a prime mover to supply alternating-current power to an output bus;

coupling a direct-current energy storage device to the output bus through a bidirectional AC/DC converter;

detecting a transient condition associated with an imbalance between generated power and load demand on the output bus;

in response to a detected excess-energy condition, operating the bidirectional AC/DC converter to transfer energy from the output bus to the direct-current energy storage device; and

in response to a detected energy-deficit condition, operating the bidirectional AC/DC converter to transfer stored energy from the direct-current energy storage device to the output bus.

The system thereby provides transient stabilization of the alternator output bus by selectively absorbing excess alternator energy and injecting stored energy to support the bus during changing load or prime-mover conditions.
